# SYNOPSIS

```text
query := expr [order by attr [asc | desc]] [limit n]
expr  := and (|| and)*
and   := not (&& not)*
not   := ! not | ( expr ) | term
term  := attr op literal | attr in lo..<hi | attr in lo...hi
       | attr in [literal, ...]
op    := == | != | < | <= | > | >= | ~=
```

# DESCRIPTION

A query names the files whose attributes match it (taisce(4)), as fs(4)'s `query` and `live` take it.
It is a superset of BFS's infix queries, so `((MAIL:status=="New")&&(Audio:Year>1990))` reads as it did there.

An attribute is a name, such as `Audio:Artist`, or one of a node's own fields: `name`, `size` or `mtime`.

A literal is one of:

- a string in double quotes, in which `*` matches any run of characters and `?` any one (`\*` is a star);
- an integer, optionally with a unit: `KiB`, `MiB`, `GiB` and `TiB` count in 1024s, `KB`, `MB`, `GB` and `TB` in 1000s;
- a decimal;
- a time, UTC, as `2026-10-09`, `2026-10-09T14:00` or `2026-10-09T14:00:30Z`;
- `true` or `false`.

`~=` compares strings ignoring case.
`in lo..<hi` is a range without its end and `in lo...hi` with it; `in [a, b, c]` is any of a list.
`order by` sorts the matches by an attribute, and `limit` keeps the first <n>.

At least one term must be answered through a declared index on its attribute; a query with none fails with `needsIndex`, unless the caller asks for a scan.

# EXAMPLES

```text
Audio:Year in 1990..<2000 && Audio:Artist ~= "*smith*"
size > 4MiB && mtime >= 2026-10-01 order by size desc limit 10
```

